square was born in st  louis missouri of all places founded by  jack dorsey  there are some other people involved but  he was the principal st louis missouri  in 2009  and during that time as the history goes  they were coming  in to introduce a simple financial  product  it was the square reader remember a  little reader that folks could plug in  to their phone  like a limo driver or independent  business people consultants  swipe a credit card and trade money for  a transaction with you and me  maybe you're buying baseball cards from  them maybe they're a simple entrepreneur  maybe they're at the county fair selling  t-shirts it allowed  the point of sale to be in the palm of  their hand anywhere  and everywhere so they started there  where'd they get their money well it was  traditional venture capital  they had a series a about 10 million  dollars in november of 09  from coastal ventures a premier uh very  environmentally focused  venture capital firm but also with a lot  of uh finance and security investments  then a 27 million series b remember abc  that's the order of which you raise  venture capital they came from sequoia  january of 11.  so so far everything's going along  pretty typically  and then suddenly they pop a hundred  million dollar series c in july of 11  led by kleiner perkins now you got  kleiner sequoia cosa and jack dorsey  we all knew this was going to be  something it was going to be something  big  because these are the biggest of the vcs  are now putting nine  digits in play they move forward and  in 2012 a  series d of 200 million from a cast  of investors so now you really have seen  the company the company is often running  and it's moving and you've got the the  cash app has been introduced and you  also have the square reader  so now they're looking at well basically  you're just a credit card processor and  you're taking a percentage that was kind  of what paypal did  they allowed you to buy things on the  internet and they took a percent well  there was more to it  and as you'll see jack dorsey was  investing and  acquiring companies as a matter of fact  over the course of square  from 2009 to date they have actually  acquired  19 different companies culminating in  the 2020 i think it was  50 million wasn't it paul they paid for  credit karma  50 million that's right so now you've  got credit karma acquired with square  you're opening up a pallet of small  business and consumer and when i say  consumer  consumers like you and me that operate  small businesses so you have  smbs like formal businesses with the  storefront and things like that  offering retail services the familiar  square point of sale  and also the ability to spin it around  you add a tip sign your name  with your finger i mean really slick  system but in addition  you still have mobility as i said limo  drivers people that have remote  businesses  matter of fact over the weekend the biz  doc babe and i went and saw  some wonderful stevie rayvon cover band  here in texas  and they were selling t-shirts for their  band and the woman there that  represented the band  she had a square reader and she had a  square  signature tab so it was really really  kind of cool to still see that going on  knowing i was going to do the case study  now  the point is that's where it started but  with these acquisitions  a lot more happened so here you go they  start cash app  in 2013. that was a big deal that's  where people like you and me could trade  cash with one another  not just have to do almost like a credit  card retail transaction  i could lend you a hundred dollars then  they announced square capital in 2014  and then an interesting thing happened  between 13 and 14.  see these here this is the revenue  numbers now i'm going to take a pause  right now and run through the revenue  numbers real quick but they crossed half  a billion dollars  in revenue so that means they're  suddenly doing 40 million  dollars a month which is over  a million dollars a day so suddenly this  is a very very  real company uh and if anybody doubted  its ability to get uh trajectory and  altitude and financial services industry  where everything is spelled with a  billion not million  financial services it's all about the  billions not the millions  so all of a sudden they're at 850  and they actually cross the magic  billion mark halfway between 14 and 15  and then toward the end of 15 no  surprise november  of 2015 to have their ipo  now what's interesting if you step back  here there's a little bit of controversy  around the ipo that i'll talk about  so when you're raising venture capital  they put a valuation on your company  that you mutually agree  sometimes you can look at comps or  comparatives of other companies that are  out there  sometimes you can't and it's what the  venture capitalists and you  wrestle to determine the valuation of  your company well when they did that 200  million dollar series d  they had a valuation of three billion  dollars so they are in the unicorn club  three times three billion dollars they  then raised about 100 million in debt in  early 14 i think it was and then  in october of 14 they raised 150 million  in a series e  now there's an interesting thing here  why would you raise a hundred million  dollars in debt well else it all has to  do with dilution  because that was a six billion dollar  valuation when they raised the last 150  million in the series e  which would be the last of the venture  funded phase for square  now why should you wait and raise that i  think there's a small lesson here  whenever you can extend the time from  one financing round to the other  and your business is on a good  trajectory you are decreasing the  percent of the business you're going to  give up in the next round  meaning your business will be more  valuable and if you let's say your  business is worth  300 million pick a big number and  somebody invests  100 million into it the 300 plus a 100  is 400 million  so therefore that 100 is 25  so that investor or the group of  investors would have 25  of it now you can roll this all the way  down to make it 3 million you took 1  million and they had 25  the same principles apply but the longer  you can wait when you're developing your  product and developing traction and  developing customers and developing  revenue  the better it is so that you give up a  less percent next time  and still people want to invest  especially if you're taking off  thanks for watching the short clip if  you like that and like to see the whole  episode  you can find it right here and for even  more  subscribe to this channel right here

